namespace gismo {
/**

\page gsAdaptiveParametrization Adaptive parametrization (r-adaptivity via a composed sigma map)

<!-- DRAFT: raw material parked from headers on 2026-09-02; polish after merge to dev -->

\section adaptparam_overview Overview

The composite map is \f$G=S\circ\sigma\f$, with \f$S\f$ a fixed geometry (or
analytic function) and \f$\sigma:[0,1]^2\to[0,1]^2\f$ a free-form
reparametrization of the reference square, represented by a \a gsSquareDomain.
Moving sigma's controls relocates the mesh in the physical domain without
touching \f$S\f$'s own control net -- this is r-adaptivity.

All optimization problems over sigma's controls derive from \a gsOptSigma,
which owns the design-variable bookkeeping (composition(), fillCurDesign(),
setCtrls(), m_domain, m_curDesign/m_numDesignVars) shared by every objective,
independent of what is being optimised.

Three families of objective build on it:
- \a gsOptMesh (the R step): a Winslow/monitor-based mesh-relocation energy,
  with an always-on Garanzha regulariser inside its own denominator. It never
  builds an additive \a gsFoldBarrier: the barrier was originally added to
  license coarsening the integration mesh, but the sampling-free coefficient
  certificate \a gsSquareDomain::minDetJCoefficient() supersedes that
  argument, and the regulariser already inside the R-step energy is always on.
- \a gsOptFit and \a gsOptL2 (the D step): a direct point-cloud fit and an
  L2-projection-error objective respectively, each owning a
  \c gsFoldBarrier<T> \c m_barrier and forwarding to it from their
  objective/gradient bodies for the fold term and a soft box penalty on the
  controls.
- \a gsAdaptiveParametrization / \a gsAdaptiveParametrizationNewton: drivers
  that own a \a gsOptMesh and solve it, either via a generic \a gsOptimizer
  (L-BFGS et al.) or via a hand-rolled Newton/Picard iteration.

"R step"/"D step" are this implementation's own names for these stages, not
terminology from the paper.

\section adaptparam_rstep R step energy (gsOptMesh)

The domain chain is \f$ \hat\Omega \xrightarrow{\sigma(\cdot;\alpha)}
\tilde\Omega \xrightarrow{G} \Omega \f$, with composed Jacobian
\f$ J_c = J_g\,J_\sigma \f$ and metric tensor \f$ C = J_c^\top J_c \f$.

**Without monitor** (\c m_fun == nullptr, \f$\omega=1/g\f$, the paper's
no-monitor energy):
\f[
  E(\alpha) = \int_{\hat\Omega} \operatorname{tr}(C^{-1}) \; d\hat\Omega
            = \int_{\hat\Omega} \frac{T}{g^2} \; d\hat\Omega
\f]
This is the harmonic-map Dirichlet energy of the inverse parameterization
\f$G^{-1}:\Omega\to\hat\Omega\f$ (Radó–Kneser–Choquet bijectivity theory).

**With monitor** (\c m_fun != nullptr, the paper's monitor-weighted energy,
surface/planar forms):
\f[
  E(\alpha) = \int_{\hat\Omega} \omega(f)\,\frac{T}{g} \; d\hat\Omega
\f]
The discrete integrand uses \f$ \omega = \sqrt{m^2} \f$ (the paper's monitor
weight, NOT \f$m^2\f$): with the fold barrier \f$\chi\f$ and (penalty\f$\to0\f$)
\f$ |\det|^3/\chi^2 \to g_\sigma \f$ this reads
\f$ \omega\,\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\,|\det|^3/\chi^2 \to \omega\,T/g \f$.
The weight \f$\omega\f$ depends on the \c MonitorMode:
- **ValueBased** (see the relevant equation): \f$ \omega = 1/\sqrt{1+\theta f} \f$,
  i.e. \f$ m^2 = 1/(1+\theta f) \f$ (linear in \f$f\f$, NOT \f$f^2\f$).
- **GradientBased** (see the relevant equation, implemented form): \f$ \omega =
  1/\sqrt{1+\theta\,\eta^2} \f$ with \f$ \eta^2 = (\nabla_\xi f)^\top
  C_g^{-1}\,(\nabla_\xi f) = \|\nabla_x f\|^2 \f$ (squared physical gradient
  norm), \f$ C_g = J_g^\top J_g \f$.

@note Surface case (td>dd): the integrand carries an extra surface area
  element \f$ g_S = \sqrt{\det C_g} \f$ so that
  \f$ \omega\,T/(g_\sigma g_S^2)\cdot g_S = \omega\,T/g_c \f$ matches the
  surface form exactly (\f$g_c = g_\sigma g_S\f$ is the composite area
  element). Planar (td==dd) needs no such factor (\f$\det J_c\f$ already
  carries the full area).

@note The \f$\det^2\f$ (no-monitor) vs \f$\det^3\f$ (with-monitor) exponent
  tracks MONITOR PRESENCE, not planar-vs-surface.

Discretely, \c evalObj computes \f$T=\operatorname{tr}(J_c^\top J_c) =
\|J_c\|_F^2\f$ DIRECTLY, not via \f$\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\f$ with a
Tikhonov-shifted \f$C\f$: shifting \f$C \mathrel{+}= \text{penalty}\cdot I\f$
destroys the exact identity \f$\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\det^2 = T\f$, so the
\f$|\det|^p\f$ factor is no longer cancelled and the integrand COLLAPSES TO 0
as \f$\det\to0\f$ -- a degenerate element then costs LESS than a perfect one
and the optimiser is rewarded for collapsing the mesh (observed: energy
1.71 -> 0.0066 while min det J_sigma -> 6e-12). Using \f$T\f$ directly removes
both the inverse and the shift, and makes the barrier exact.

The fold barrier instead regularises the composite area element with the
Garanzha regulariser \f$\chi(x) = 0.5\,(x + \sqrt{\varepsilon^2+x^2})\f$:
- Planar (td==dd): \f$g_{\mathrm{reg}} = \chi(\det J_c)\f$ (\f$g_c=|\det J_c|\f$).
- Surface (td>dd): \f$g_{\mathrm{reg}} = \chi(\det J_\sigma)\,g_S\f$
  (\f$g_c=|\det J_\sigma|\,g_S\f$, \f$g_S=\sqrt{\det(J_g^\top J_g)}\f$ the
  source area element; only \f$\det J_\sigma\f$ can change sign in the
  surface case, so the barrier acts on it).

Then \f$E = \int T/g_{\mathrm{reg}}^2\f$ (no monitor) or \f$\int \omega\,T/g_{\mathrm{reg}}\f$
(with monitor). As \f$\varepsilon\to0\f$ these reproduce
\f$\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\f$ resp. \f$\omega\,T/g_c\f$, but unlike the
\f$|\det|^p/\chi^q\f$ form they are MONOTONE into degeneracy: at \f$\det=0\f$
they equal \f$4T/\varepsilon^2\f$ resp. \f$2\,\omega\,T/\varepsilon\f$ (huge),
and grow without bound for \f$\det<0\f$. Degeneracy is penalised, not rewarded.

The "Penalty" option is this Garanzha regulariser; it is always on (no
additive fold barrier is layered on top -- see \ref adaptparam_overview).

Per-thread partials in the OpenMP element sweep are summed in THREAD-ID
order (not as threads finish), making evalObj bit-reproducible for a given
thread count and hence the whole R step reproducible run-to-run.

\section adaptparam_rgrad R step analytic gradient

Differentiates the integrand of evalObj() with respect to each control
variable \f$ \alpha_{k,d} \f$ (the \a d-th coordinate of the \a k-th basis
function coefficient of \f$\sigma\f$).

Notation: \f$\alpha_{k,d}\f$: d-th coordinate of the k-th basis function
coefficient; \f$N_k\f$: k-th basis function of sigma at the quadrature
point; \f$\nabla_{\hat u} N_k\f$: gradient of \f$N_k\f$ w.r.t. the reference
coordinates; \f$\xi=\sigma(\hat u;\alpha)\f$: parametric point in
\f$\tilde\Omega\f$; \f$G(\xi)\f$: geometry map \f$\tilde\Omega\to\Omega\f$;
\f$J_\sigma\f$ (dd x dd), \f$J_g\f$ (td x dd), \f$J_c=J_g J_\sigma\f$ (td x
dd); \f$C=J_c^\top J_c\f$ (dd x dd); \f$C_g=J_g^\top J_g\f$ (dd x dd);
\f$\det_c=\det(J_c)\f$ (square case td==dd); \f$\chi_c=0.5(\det_c+
\sqrt{\text{penalty}^2+\det_c^2})\f$.

Kinematic derivatives:
\f{align*}{
  \frac{\partial\xi_i}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}} &= N_k\,\delta_{id}, \\
  \frac{\partial J_\sigma}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}}
    &= e_d\,(\nabla_{\hat u} N_k)^\top
    \quad\text{(only row \a d nonzero)}, \\
  \frac{\partial J_g}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}}(a,j)
    &= N_k \frac{\partial^2 G_a}{\partial\xi_j\,\partial\xi_d}
    \quad\text{(second derivative of \f$G\f$, contracted with }d\xi/d\alpha=N_k e_d\text{)}, \\
  \frac{\partial J_c}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}} &= \frac{\partial J_g}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}} J_\sigma
    + J_g \frac{\partial J_\sigma}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}}, \\
  \frac{\partial C}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}} &= \Big(\frac{\partial J_c}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}}\Big)^\top J_c
    + J_c^\top \frac{\partial J_c}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}}.
\f}

Determinant derivative (planar, td==dd):
\f[
  \frac{\partial \det_c}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}} = \operatorname{tr}\!\Big(\operatorname{adj}(J_c)^\top
    \frac{\partial J_c}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}}\Big)
  = N_k\,\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{adj}(J_c)\,D_d\,J_\sigma)
  + \operatorname{adj}(J_c)^\top_{\cdot,d}\cdot\nabla_{\hat u}N_k,
\f]
with \f$D_d(a,j)=\partial^2 G_a/(\partial\xi_j\partial\xi_d)\f$; the two
terms come from the \f$\partial J_g\f$ and \f$\partial J_\sigma\f$
contributions to \f$\partial J_c\f$ respectively.

Objective and integrand: \f$\phi_2=|\det_c|^2/\chi_c^2\f$ (no-monitor
exponent \f$p=2\f$, \f$E\approx T/g^2\f$), \f$\phi_3=|\det_c|^3/\chi_c^2\f$
(with-monitor exponent \f$p=3\f$, \f$E\approx m^2 T/g\f$). No-monitor:
\f$E_{\mathrm{nm}}=\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\,\phi_2\f$; with-monitor:
\f$E_{\mathrm{wm}}=m^2\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\,\phi_3\f$. Both integrands
blow up as \f$\det_c\to0\f$ (fold), providing a fold barrier. (The energy
actually assembled by evalObj(), \f$T/\chi_c^2\f$ resp. \f$\omega T/\chi_c\f$,
is the equivalent unshifted form described in \ref adaptparam_rstep; this
\f$\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\phi_p\f$ form is the one this gradient
differentiates.)

No-monitor integrand derivative:
\f[
  dE_{\mathrm{nm}} = -\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1}\,dC\,C^{-1})\,\phi_2
    + \operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\,d\phi_2, \qquad
  d\phi_2 = \phi_2\Big(\frac{2}{\det_c} - \frac{2\,d\chi_c/d\det_c}{\chi_c}\Big)\,d\det_c.
\f]

With-monitor integrand derivative (both ValueBased and GradientBased):
\f[
  dE_{\mathrm{wm}} = dm^2\,\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\,\phi_3
    + m^2\Big(-\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1}\,dC\,C^{-1})\,\phi_3 + \operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\,d\phi_3\Big),
  \qquad
  d\phi_3 = \phi_3\Big(\frac{3}{\det_c} - \frac{2\,d\chi_c/d\det_c}{\chi_c}\Big)\,d\det_c.
\f]

\f$dm^2 = (dm^2/d\eta^2)(d\eta^2/d\alpha)\f$:
- **ValueBased**: \f$\eta=f(\xi(\alpha))\f$, \f$m^2=1/(1+\theta\eta^2)\f$,
  \f$dm^2/d\eta=-2\theta\eta/(1+\theta\eta^2)^2\f$,
  \f$d\eta/d\alpha_{k,d}=(\nabla_\xi f)_d\,N_k\f$ (chain rule).
- **GradientBased**: \f$\eta^2=(\nabla_\xi f)^\top C_g^{-1}(\nabla_\xi f)\f$,
  \f$m^2=1/(1+\theta\eta^2)\f$, \f$dm^2/d(\eta^2)=-\theta/(1+\theta\eta^2)^2\f$.
  \f$d(\eta^2)/d\alpha_{k,d}\f$ has two terms: from \f$C_g\f$ changing, with
  \f$v=C_g^{-1}\nabla_\xi f\f$, \f$\text{term}_1=-v^\top dC_g\,v\f$
  (\f$dC_g = dJ_g^\top J_g + J_g^\top dJ_g\f$); from \f$\nabla_\xi f\f$
  changing at the moving point, \f$\text{term}_2=2v^\top d(\nabla_\xi f)\f$,
  with \f$d(\nabla_\xi f)/d\alpha_{k,d}=N_k H_f e_d\f$ if \c parametric
  (\f$H_f\f$ the dd x dd parametric Hessian of \f$f\f$), or
  \f$d(\nabla_\xi f)/d\alpha_{k,d}=dJ_g^\top\nabla_x f + N_k J_g^\top H_f
  J_g e_d\f$ if not (\f$H_f\f$ the td x td physical Hessian, \f$J_g e_d\f$
  the \a d-th column of \f$J_g\f$).

**Surface case (td>dd)**: \f$J_c\f$ is rectangular, so there is no scalar
\f$\det(J_c)\f$; the fold barrier instead acts on \f$\det_s=\det(J_\sigma)\f$
(the only quantity that can change sign, since a fold of sigma is what a
surface parametrization must forbid), with \f$\chi_s=0.5(\det_s+
\sqrt{\text{penalty}^2+\det_s^2})\f$ mirroring the planar case, and \f$g_S=
\sqrt{\det(J_g^\top J_g)}\f$ the SOURCE area element entering both branches
(not just the monitor branch), since it enters \f$g_{\mathrm{reg}}\f$
directly. \c gsOptL2's planar/surface gradient keeps the SIGNED
\f$\det(J_S)\f$ in the planar case and the non-negative area element
\f$\sqrt{\det(J_S^\top J_S)}\f$ in the surface case; \f$\det(J_\sigma)\f$
itself stays SIGNED in both cases -- the fold barrier is what keeps it
positive, not the objective.

\section adaptparam_dstep D step: gsOptFit and gsOptL2

**gsOptFit**: a variable-projection objective with the geometry \f$S\f$
fixed, minimizing the TRUE least-squares objective
\f[
  E(\alpha) = \frac1N \sum_i \| S(\sigma(uv_i;\alpha)) - x_i \|^2
            + \mu \sum_q w_q \max(0,\varepsilon-\det J_\sigma(u_q))^2
            + \mu \sum_j \big(\max(0,-\alpha_j)^2+\max(0,\alpha_j-1)^2\big),
\f]
where the last two terms (fold barrier, soft box penalty) are supplied by
\a m_barrier (a \a gsFoldBarrier) via addObj()/addGrad() -- \a gsOptFit adds
only the data term. Analytic gradient: with \f$r_i = S(\xi_i)-x_i\f$ and free
control \f$\alpha_{(k,d)}\f$ (per \a gsSquareDomain's mapper),
\f[
  \frac{\partial E}{\partial \alpha_{(k,d)}} =
    \frac2N \sum_i \big(r_i^\top J_S(\xi_i)\big)_d \, N_k(uv_i),
\f]
where the collocation values \f$N_k(uv_i)\f$ are fixed throughout the
optimization and precomputed once as a sparse matrix (m_colloc).

**gsOptL2**: an L2-projection-error objective, with the analysis-space
(projection) coefficients FROZEN. The geometry map is the composed map
\f$G=S\circ\sigma\f$ with \a S fixed; the frozen discrete solution \f$u_h\f$
is a function of the REFERENCE variable \f$v\in\hat\Omega\f$ (i.e. it lives
on the plain, non-composed analysis/projection basis). This is what makes
the objective well posed: moving \f$\sigma\f$ genuinely moves the physical
point \f$G(v)\f$ that \f$u_h(v)\f$ is compared against, while \f$u_h(v)\f$
itself stays fixed (envelope theorem: the coefficients that minimize the L2
error do not need to be re-projected for a first-order-accurate gradient of
the error at the projected optimum).

@warning Had the analysis/projection basis instead been COMPOSED with
  \f$\sigma\f$ too, a change of variables would make this frozen-coefficient
  error \f$\sigma\f$-INDEPENDENT and the gradient identically zero -- a
  silent no-op. \a gsOptL2 exists to avoid exactly that trap.

Objective (mu=0 shown; barrier/box from m_barrier):
\f[
  E(\alpha) = \int_{\hat\Omega} \big(u_h(v) - f(G(v;\alpha))\big)^2
              \, g_S(\xi)\,\det J_\sigma(v;\alpha) \; dv,
\f]
with \f$G=S\circ\sigma_\alpha\f$, \f$\xi=\sigma(v)\f$, and \f$g_S\f$ the area
element of \f$S\f$ at \f$\xi\f$: for a planar \a S (\f$\mathrm{td}=
\mathrm{dd}=2\f$) \f$g_S=\det J_S(\xi)\f$ (SIGNED); for a surface \a S
(\f$\mathrm{td}>\mathrm{dd}\f$) \f$g_S=\sqrt{\det(J_S(\xi)^\top J_S(\xi))}
\ge 0\f$, the usual \f$\sqrt{\det C_g}\f$ surface-measure factor with
\f$C_g=J_S^\top J_S\f$. At the controls that produced a given
\a f-projection's coefficients, this \f$E\f$ (mu=0) numerically reproduces
the value \c gsL2Projection::project returns for the same \a S, sigma,
\a f, analysis basis and integration basis (planar case).

Analytic gradient: let \f$N_k\f$ be sigma's basis functions,
\f$\xi=\sigma(v)\f$, \f$J_\sigma\f$, \f$J_S=J_S(\xi)\f$,
\f$r(v)=u_h(v)-F(\xi)\f$ with \f$F(\xi)=f(S(\xi))\f$ if \c parametric is
\c false (the default -- \a fun is a function of the PHYSICAL point) or
\f$F=f\f$ if \c parametric is \c true (\a fun is a function of \f$\xi\f$),
and \f$w(v)=g_S\,\det(J_\sigma)\f$. Then \f$E=\int r^2 w\,dv\f$ and, with
\f$d\xi_i/d\alpha_{k,d}=N_k\delta_{id}\f$,
\f[
  \frac{\partial E}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}} =
    \int \Big[ -2\,r\cdot\frac{\partial F}{\partial\xi_d}\,N_k\,w
               + r^2 \Big( \partial_d(g_S)\,N_k\,\det J_\sigma
                           + g_S \cdot
                             \frac{\partial(\det J_\sigma)}{\partial\alpha_{k,d}}
                    \Big) \Big] \; dv,
\f]
where \f$\partial F/\partial\xi = J_S^\top \nabla_x f\f$ (parametric ==
false) or the direct parametric gradient of \a fun (parametric == true);
\f$D_d(a,j)=\partial^2 S_a/(\partial\xi_j\partial\xi_d)\f$ (from \a S's
second derivatives); \f$\partial_d(g_S)\f$ takes two forms:
- planar: \f$\partial_d(g_S) = \operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{adj}(J_S)\,D_d)\f$;
- surface: \f$\partial_d(g_S) = \tfrac12\,g_S\,
  \operatorname{tr}(C_g^{-1} E_d)\f$ with
  \f$E_d = D_d^\top J_S + J_S^\top D_d\f$;

and \f$\partial(\det J_\sigma)/\partial\alpha_{k,d}\f$ is exactly the
quantity \a gsSquareDomain::detJacobianDeriv_into computes for the fold
barrier, already indexed by free control (no further scatter needed for
that term).

@note \f$\mathrm{dd}=2\f$ is hard-wired for both sweeps (sigma is always the
  unit square); \a S may be planar (\f$\mathrm{td}=2\f$, SIGNED
  \f$\det J_S\f$) or a surface (\f$\mathrm{td}>2\f$, non-negative
  \f$g_S=\sqrt{\det(J_S^\top J_S)}\f$); \f$\det J_\sigma\f$ itself stays
  SIGNED in both cases -- the fold barrier is what keeps it positive, not
  this objective.

\section adaptparam_foldbarrier gsFoldBarrier

Owns the sigma map (a non-owning pointer), mu, eps, and whatever per-mode
data the chosen \a gsFoldBarrierMode needs to evaluate the barrier and its
analytic gradient without repeating a basis evaluation or a spline-arithmetic
construction on every optimizer iteration:
- \a Sampled caches a Gauss rule on sigma's own knot mesh and sigma's basis
  derivatives at those points.
- \a Coefficient caches the two EXACT linear operators that extract
  d(sigma)/du and d(sigma)/dv in full Bezier form from sigma's control
  coefficients, plus the two binomial weight tables of the Leibniz product
  det(J_sigma) = d_u(sigma_0) d_v(sigma_1) - d_v(sigma_0) d_u(sigma_1). Both
  are exact fixed linear maps of sigma's control coefficients (no
  interpolation solve anywhere); since sigma's basis is a tensor product,
  each extraction operator itself factors into a Kronecker product of two
  1-D maps, so the cache holds only the four 1-D factors -- O(nu+nv) storage
  and setup instead of the O(nu*nv) a directly-formed 2-D operator would
  need -- and a per-iteration evaluation reduces to a handful of small dense
  mat-mats and a per-element binomial convolution.

@note Per-call cost: \a Coefficient mode's addObj()/addGrad()/
  minDetJacobian() never call a basis evaluation, a quadrature rule or any
  spline-arithmetic factory routine -- everything that needs one is
  precomputed in initCoefficient(). \a Sampled mode is a verbatim port of the
  original Gauss-quadrature barrier and is NOT held to that bar: its
  addGrad() calls \a gsSquareDomain::detJacobianDeriv_into(), which
  internally does call \c basis().compute() -- unavoidable there, since the
  barrier points are fixed but sigma's coefficients (and hence the basis
  contraction) change every iteration.

**Kronecker/Leibniz derivation (Coefficient mode).** With sigma of bidegree
(p,q), \f$d_u(\sigma_c)\f$ has bidegree (p-1,q) and \f$d_v(\sigma_c)\f$ has
bidegree (p,q-1). \c grad()+toBezier() are EXACT fixed linear maps of
sigma's control coefficients, so
\f[
  a = A_u X \;(d_u\sigma_0), \quad a' = A_v X \;(d_v\sigma_0), \quad
  b = A_u Y \;(d_u\sigma_1), \quad b' = A_v Y \;(d_v\sigma_1),
\f]
with \f$A_u, A_v\f$ depending only on sigma's knot vectors (independent of
the current coefficients \f$X\f$=sigma's x-coefficients, \f$Y\f$=sigma's
y-coefficients). Sigma's basis is a plain tensor product, and both d/du
(resp. d/dv) and Bezier extraction act direction-by-direction with no
cross-direction coupling, so each 2-D extraction operator is EXACTLY a
Kronecker product of two 1-D factors, never formed as a 2-D matrix:
\f[
  A_u = E_v \otimes D_u, \qquad A_v = F_v \otimes C_u,
\f]
with \f$D_u/F_v\f$ the 1-D differentiate+Bezier-extract factor in u/v and
\f$C_u/E_v\f$ the 1-D Bezier-extract-only factor in u/v
(\c gsFoldBarrierDeriv1D/\c gsFoldBarrierBezier1D port \c grad(dir)'s
formula and \c toBezier()'s knot-insertion loop to a single direction;
\c gsBSpline<T> itself has no matching grad()/toBezier() pair -- its
toBezier() returns a gsMultiPatch of split segments, a different
representation). Each factor has O(nel*deg) rows and O(nu or nv) columns --
O(n) storage and setup instead of the O(nu*nv) a directly-formed \f$A_u,A_v\f$
would need.

Applying a Kronecker factor to sigma's nu x nv coefficient grid \f$X_{mat}\f$
(column-major, u fastest) is TWO small dense products, the Kronecker product
itself never formed:
\f[
  A_{mat} = D_u\,X_{mat}\,E_v^\top \qquad ((\mathrm{nelU}\cdot\mathrm{degU})\times(\mathrm{nelV}\cdot(\mathrm{degV}+1)))
\f]
and the adjoint (used by addCoefficientGrad(), \f$(A\otimes B)^\top = A^\top
\otimes B^\top\f$ combined with \f$\operatorname{vec}(PMQ^\top) = (Q\otimes
P)\operatorname{vec}(M)\f$) is the same shape transposed:
\f[
  G_{mat} = D_u^\top\,M_{u,mat}\,E_v.
\f]
computeExtraction()/addCoefficientGrad() apply exactly this.

The Leibniz product \f$\det = a b' - a' b\f$ is the SAME per-element
Bernstein convolution \c gsTensorBSpline::multiply(.,.,keepBezier=true) uses
internally, applied directly to the cached \f$a,a',b,b'\f$ fields instead of
re-deriving them from scratch -- no basis evaluation, no spline arithmetic,
only small dense products and a small per-element double sum, entirely
inside addObj()/addGrad()/minDetJacobian().

Every interior knot of a Bezier-form spline sits at multiplicity degree+1,
so in direction d a spline of degree \f$q_d\f$ over \f$\mathrm{nel}_d\f$
elements has exactly \f$\mathrm{nel}_d(q_d+1)\f$ coefficients laid out in
standard tensor lexicographic order (i_0 fastest), with element \f$e_d\f$
owning the CONTIGUOUS block \f$[e_d(q_d+1),(e_d+1)(q_d+1))\f$ -- exactly the
layout \c gsTensorBSpline::multiply's own element loop assumes, and exactly
what column-major reshaping \f$A_{mat}\f$ as a flat vector gives, u fastest.
This closed-form indexing is what lets the per-element convolution run with
no lookup structure at all: computeCoefficients()/addCoefficientGrad() are
UNCHANGED by the Kronecker factorisation, reading m_aFull/m_bFull/m_apFull/
m_bpFull via this flat index, oblivious to how those buffers were filled.

Member-cache summary (\a Coefficient mode): sigma's degrees and
per-direction element counts are cached for this closed-form indexing;
m_Du/m_Ev/m_Cu/m_Fv are the four 1-D Kronecker factors; m_w1u/m_w1v/m_w2u/
m_w2v are the per-direction binomial weight tables of the Leibniz product's
two terms (term 1 is \f$ab'\f$, operand bidegrees (degU-1,degV) x
(degU,degV-1); term 2 is \f$a'b\f$, operand bidegrees (degU,degV-1) x
(degU-1,degV) -- the SAME pair of bidegrees with the roles swapped, which is
why the weight differs between the two terms even though both results share
the bidegree (2degU-1,2degV-1). Reusing one table for both would be a
silent wrong answer whenever degU != degV or the two directions are refined
differently).

\section adaptparam_foldcert gsSquareDomain fold certification

**minJacobian(nPerElement)**: minimum of det(J_sigma), sampled on an
\a nPerElement^d point grid PER ELEMENT of sigma's knot mesh. The sampling
density is the CALLER's policy, deliberately: this is a reporting/diagnostic
routine with no library-internal callers, and a single hard-wired grid
undersamples fine sigma meshes. A density below 2 samples per direction is
rejected: 0 would sample nothing (returning the numeric_limits sentinel
unchanged) and 1 degenerates to each element's lower corner, both silently
plausible-looking wrong minima. This is a NECESSARY but never SUFFICIENT
fold check: a negative result proves a fold (a sample point saw it), but a
positive result proves nothing -- a fold hiding between sample points is
invisible to it.

**detJacobianSpline(keepBezier)**: represents det(J_sigma) EXACTLY (to
round-off) as a scalar tensor B-spline, in the one spline space that
actually contains it. Sigma is a tensor B-spline of degree \f$p_d\f$ in
direction d. Writing \f$J(c,j)=\partial\sigma_c/\partial\xi_j\f$,
det(J_sigma) is built as the exact Bernstein product of the Jacobian's
entries: each \f$J(c,j)\f$ is itself a scalar tensor B-spline
(\c gsTensorBSpline::grad), and det(J_sigma) is their Leibniz expansion,
formed via \c gsTensorBSpline::multiply and
\c gsTensorBSpline::linearCombination. The result has degree \f$2p_d-1\f$
per direction d (domainDim() factors, one differentiated per term). With
\a keepBezier == false (the default), \c multiply compresses each product to
its structural minimal continuity space; with \a keepBezier == true the raw
(uncompressed) Bezier form is returned instead -- the tightest possible
coefficient bound for minDetJCoefficient(), at the cost of a larger basis.
Because the construction is exact spline arithmetic (no degree/continuity
precondition on sigma), this works for a bilinear (\f$p_d=1\f$) sigma as
well: the product is then a legal, discontinuous (\f$C^{-1}\f$) spline space
that still contains det(J_sigma) exactly.

**minDetJCoefficient()**: guaranteed LOWER BOUND on det(J_sigma) over the
whole domain, computed WITHOUT sampling anywhere. The tensor B-spline basis
is non-negative and forms a partition of unity, so with
\f$\det(J_\sigma) = \sum_i c_i B_i(x)\f$, \f$B_i(x)\ge0\f$ and
\f$\sum_i B_i(x)=1\f$ for every x, immediately \f$\det(J_\sigma)(x)\ge\min_i
c_i\f$ for every x in the domain. This method returns that \f$\min_i c_i\f$,
i.e. \c detJacobianSpline()->coefs().minCoeff(). Because it is exact and
needs no evaluation grid, it certifies what a sampled check never can:
- a **positive** minDetJCoefficient() PROVES sigma is fold-free everywhere,
  including between any sample points minJacobian(n) might have used;
- a **negative** minDetJCoefficient() proves NOTHING by itself (the bound
  may simply be conservative) -- compare against minJacobian(n) or a dense
  grid to tell an actual fold from conservatism;
- conversely, a **negative** minJacobian(n) PROVES a fold (a sample point
  saw it directly);
- a **positive** minJacobian(n) proves NOTHING (a fold between sample points
  is invisible to it) -- this is exactly the gap minDetJCoefficient() closes.

Ordering that always holds:
\code minDetJCoefficient() <= true min_x det(J_sigma) <= minJacobian(nPerElement) \endcode

This is a single-pass compute(): without overriding \c compute(), \c
gsFunctionSet::compute() falls back to calling eval_into()/deriv_into()/
deriv2_into() in turn, and each of those delegates to the underlying
B-spline geometry SEPARATELY -- so asking for value+derivative evaluates the
sigma basis twice, and value+derivative+second derivative three times.
Delegating to \c gsGeometry::compute() instead runs the basis'
evalAllDers_into() once and contracts each requested order with the control
points. This matters because gsOptMesh's objective and gradient sweeps call
compute() on this object for every element on every optimizer iteration,
making it a hot-path routine on which redundant basis evaluation is directly
visible in wall time.

\section adaptparam_newton Newton relocation

**Why no Tikhonov shift.** As in the R step (\ref adaptparam_rstep), the
Newton/Picard assembly never shifts \f$C\mathrel{+}=\text{penalty}\cdot I\f$:
that would destroy the exact identity
\f$\operatorname{tr}(C^{-1})\det^2 = T\f$ and make the integrand collapse to
0 as \f$\det\to0\f$, i.e. it would REWARD mesh collapse. \c gsOptMesh
assembles the same unregularised energy, and this path must match it. Known,
accepted hazard: \f$C\f$ is inverted unregularised, so at a fold
(\f$\det(J_c)\le0\f$ somewhere in the mesh) \f$C\f$ becomes numerically
singular and this path returns NaN residual/Hessian, while \c gsOptMesh
stays finite (it never inverts \f$C\f$). The Newton driver must therefore
never be started from, or allowed to step into, a folded iterate.

**q_exp.** With the Tikhonov shift removed, \f$\operatorname{tr}(Q)\det^2=T\f$
exactly (\f$Q=C^{-1}\f$), so the barrier exponent belongs on \f$\chi\f$ (not
on \f$|\det|\f$) to reproduce gsOptMesh's \f$T/\chi^2\f$ (no monitor) and
\f$\omega T/\chi\f$ (with monitor): \f$q_{\mathrm{exp}}=1\f$ with a monitor,
\f$2\f$ without.

\section adaptparam_supermesh makeIntegrationBasis: the super mesh

**Degree derivation.** The integration basis is the parent (analysis) basis
with sigma's knots inserted, then degree-raised per direction to
\f$\mathrm{targetDegree}=p_{\mathrm{analysis}}\cdot p_\sigma\f$. This degree
is NOT an exact quadrature degree for \f$G(\sigma(u))\f$ in general: sigma
couples the parametric directions, so substituting \f$x_i=\sigma_i(u)\f$
into a monomial \f$\prod_i x_i^{a_i}\f$ (\f$a_i\le p_G\f$) contributes to the
per-direction degree of every \f$u_j\f$ simultaneously, reaching
\f$d\cdot p_G\cdot p_\sigma\f$ per direction in the worst case, not
\f$p_G\cdot p_\sigma\f$.

**Counterexample.** \f$N(x,y)=xy\f$, bidegree (1,1) with no interior knots
(so the domain is already one element for N, and knot insertion changes
nothing on the analysis side). The minimal gsSquareDomain with a free
control is bidegree (2,2), no interior knots (a 3x3 net whose only free
control is the centre, \c gsSquareDomain::_initMapper eliminates every
boundary control): \f$\sigma_1(u,v)=u+aN_1(u)N_1(v)\f$,
\f$\sigma_2(u,v)=v+bN_1(u)N_1(v)\f$, with \f$N_1(t)=2t(1-t)\f$ the middle
quadratic B-spline on \f$\{0,0,0,1,1,1\}\f$ -- the centre control displaced
from its default (0.5,0.5) to (0.5+a,0.5+b). Here \f$p_G=1\f$, \f$p_\sigma=2\f$,
so \f$\mathrm{targetDegree}=p_G p_\sigma=2\f$; but
\f$N(\sigma)=\sigma_1\sigma_2=\dots+ab\,N_1(u)^2N_1(v)^2+\dots\f$, whose
degree in \f$u\f$ is 4 (from \f$N_1(u)^2\f$), not \f$\mathrm{targetDegree}=2\f$.
That is \f$d\cdot p_G\cdot p_\sigma=2\cdot1\cdot2=4\f$, the coupled bound,
attained. The product bound \f$p_G\cdot p_\sigma\f$ is reached only for a
direction-wise sigma (each \f$\sigma_i\f$ a function of \f$u_i\f$ alone); no
condition on the analysis basis's own knots restores it, because the
obstruction is direction coupling in sigma, not knot placement in the
analysis basis.

**Consequence for quadrature.** Inserting sigma's knots still guarantees
sigma is a single polynomial piece on each union-mesh element;
\f$\mathrm{targetDegree}\f$ equals the uncoupled bound \f$p_G p_\sigma\f$
exactly -- it is the quadrature RULE sized from it that over-integrates: the
default (quA=1, quB=1) gives \f$n=\mathrm{targetDegree}+1\f$ nodes per
direction, exact to degree \f$2\,\mathrm{targetDegree}+1\f$. That is
sufficient in 2D (\f$2\,\mathrm{targetDegree}\le2\,\mathrm{targetDegree}+1\f$)
but not in 3D once \f$\mathrm{targetDegree}\ge2\f$ (worst-case per-direction
degree \f$3\,\mathrm{targetDegree}\f$ exceeds \f$2\,\mathrm{targetDegree}+1\f$):
the default rule is conservative in 2D but not in 3D once
\f$\mathrm{targetDegree}\ge2\f$. The integrand also carries \f$\det(J)\f$ and
the monitor function, neither in general polynomial of degree
\f$p_G p_\sigma\f$ (or polynomial at all), so exactness fails on that count
too regardless of dimension. In practice this over-integration is an
approximation with a measured accuracy floor around 1e-5 to 2e-4 for the
meshes and quadrature settings exercised so far in this codebase;
differences below that floor are quadrature noise, not results.

**Dyadic hierarchical union (THB overload).** Builds the super mesh on the
analysis THB's ACTUAL hierarchical element partition: element-wise level
\f$\max(\mathrm{analysisLevel},L_\sigma)\f$, degree
\f$p_{\mathrm{analysis}}(dir)\cdot p_\sigma(dir)\f$ per direction. The union
mesh is built by transplanting both partitions (the analysis hierarchy and
sigma's own mesh as one dyadic level) onto a SHARED level-0 basis B0 (the
precondition of \c gsHTensorBasis::merge()): sigma's mesh MUST be exactly a
dyadic level of the analysis hierarchy, sharing its level 0. B0 is the
shared level-0 basis, degree-raised per direction to
\f$p_{\mathrm{analysis}}\cdot p_\sigma\f$ (same target degree and
non-exactness argument as above); \c degreeIncrease raises the degree but
leaves the breakpoints -- and hence every dyadic level built on B0 --
unchanged, so B0 can be shared by the analysis-derived and sigma-derived
hierarchies. \c sigmaLevelInHierarchy() determines the smallest dyadic
refinement level of level 0 whose element count is >= sigma's, clamped at 0.
Its precondition: the analysis basis' level-0 tensor mesh must have at least
one element in every direction (true for any valid basis; a violation sets
\a reason and returns \c false rather than looping). A non-nested pair
(sigma's mesh not a dyadic level of the hierarchy) has no such common root;
use the tensor overload on the analysis basis's finest level instead.

**Pass-through constructor (integrationBasisIsFinal_t).** Stores a
caller-supplied \a integrationBasis verbatim as the integration mesh, for a
caller that already built the union itself (e.g. via the THB overload above)
and would otherwise have the degree raise applied a SECOND time by the
ordinary constructors. Validates \a integrationBasis before storing it: its
\c domainDim() must match sigma's, and -- per direction -- it must share
sigma's own parameter range exactly, have degree at least sigma's, and
contain all of sigma's interior knots. Checked for a tensor B-spline/NURBS
\a integrationBasis, or one deriving from \c gsHTensorBasis -- i.e. any
\c gsTHBSplineBasis, truncated or not: \c gsHBSplineBasis is the SAME class
template with \c Trunc=false, not a separate derived type, so this one check
covers both; \c gsRationalTHBSplineBasis derives from
\c gsRationalBasis<gsTHBSplineBasis<d,T>> instead and is NOT covered. Any
other concrete basis type, in 2D or 3D, is accepted with the domainDim check
only, since there is no cheap generic way to enumerate its degree, parameter
range or interior knots. A basis coarser than sigma's own knot mesh, of
lower degree, or on a different parameter range would otherwise be accepted
silently and then mis-integrate -- under-integrate for a coarser mesh or
lower degree, extrapolate or leave part of the domain unintegrated for a
mismatched range. For a hierarchical candidate this is checked against its
finest tensor level, deliberately NOT via \c sigmaLevelInHierarchy(): the
THB \c makeIntegrationBasis() overload GISMO_ENSUREs on that helper, i.e.
rejects any pair that is not a single dyadic level of the hierarchy -- but
here a hierarchical basis whose finest level resolves sigma is legitimate
even when it is NOT a single dyadic level of sigma's own ladder, e.g. for a
manual-levels THB or a level-0 mesh that is not a dyadic ancestor of sigma's,
even when that THB's finest level DOES contain every one of sigma's knots.
Reusing the hard check here would reject legitimate caller-supplied bases.
Checking against the finest tensor level instead is necessary (if a knot is
missing from the whole hierarchy's finest global grid, it cannot be present
anywhere) but not sufficient (a knot present in the finest GLOBAL grid does
not prove the mesh is locally that fine along that knot's entire line, e.g.
a manual-levels THB refined away from that knot elsewhere).

**Knot tolerance.** Both \c tensorBasisAdmissible() and
\c sigmaLevelInHierarchy() use an absolute tolerance (1e-12), appropriate
because sigma's basis always lives on the fixed parameter domain [0,1] in
every direction. A candidate knot that matches within tolerance is treated
as present, and the union mesh then carries the candidate's knot value, not
sigma's -- the element boundary is displaced by O(tol), harmless at 1e-12
but a real substitution.

\section adaptparam_performance Performance: per-thread scratch and reduction order

**Per-thread gsThreaded scratch.** Declaring the objective/gradient sweeps'
buffers (several gsFuncData plus a dozen Eigen objects plus a gsQuadRule)
fresh inside the `#pragma omp parallel` region would reconstruct them on
every thread on every objective evaluation. An L-BFGS solve makes
O(100-1000) evaluations and each thread only gets a handful of elements, so
that reconstruction cost is a real share of the wall time rather than a
rounding error. Holding it in a \c util::gsThreaded member instead -- the
same idiom \c gsExprHelper uses for its gsFuncData/gsMapData/points/weights
-- allocates the buffers once and reuses them for the whole solve. The
cached gsQuadRule additionally survives across calls, so the Gauss rule is
built once per patch per solve instead of once per patch per thread per
call. This rationale is shared by \c gsOptMesh::EvalScratch/GradScratch and
\c gsOptL2::EvalScratch/GradScratch.

@warning Like gsExprHelper's, each scratch array is sized with
  omp_get_max_threads() at construction; raising the thread count after the
  owning optimizer object has been built is not supported.

**Deterministic thread-id-ordered reduction.** Summing per-thread partials
as they finish (e.g. via `#pragma omp atomic update result += thResult`)
makes the objective differ in its last bits from run to run. L-BFGS
amplifies that: on one measured schedule, the SAME binary needed anywhere
from 198 to 535 iterations and returned min det J_sigma between 9.5e-2 and
1.9e-1 depending on run. Summing per-thread partials in THREAD-ID order
instead makes evalObj (and the analogous gradient/Newton assembly sweeps)
bit-reproducible for a given thread count, hence the whole R step
reproducible.

*/
}
